# 035-matrix-reaction-hook-forwarding.patch
#
# Local patch — NOT an upstream PR. Checked 2026-10-12 against upstream main
# (raw.githubusercontent.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent/main/plugins/platforms/matrix/adapter.py):
# the file is still 3267 lines, contains no `set_reaction_handler` call, no ROOM_REDACTION event
# handler, and ends `_on_reaction` on the same three built-in prompt handlers as our v0.21.6 copy,
# so the gap is still open upstream and there is no issue or PR to cite. (main's copy differs from
# ours in 34 unrelated hunks — `Dict[...]` -> `dict[...]`, `subprocess.run(..., check=False)`,
# dropped `# noqa` — none of them near these anchors.)
#
# Why: base.py documents a platform-neutral reaction contract — `set_reaction_handler` takes a
# normalised dict (platform, event_name "reaction:added"/"reaction:removed", reaction, user_id,
# item_user_id, channel_id, message_ts, event_ts, raw_event) and fans it out through
# `HookRegistry.emit`; run_adapters.py wires that setter on every adapter; Slack implements it in
# `_handle_slack_reaction`. The Matrix adapter only inherits the setter: a human reaction reaches
# `_on_reaction`, matches none of the three in-memory prompt registries (exec approval, model
# picker, choice picker) and is silently dropped — no hooks/ handler and no plugin can observe a
# Matrix reaction at all.
#
# After: an unclaimed reaction is forwarded as "reaction:added" (message_ts = the reacted-to message
# event id, event_ts = the m.reaction event id), and a new ROOM_REDACTION handler forwards
# `m.room.redaction` as "reaction:removed".
#
# Why redactions are in scope: the client-server API has no "reaction removed" event. Tapping an
# already-set reaction off is a redaction of the user's own m.reaction event and carries only that
# event's id, so a consumer that must undo on the second tap has no other signal. The removed
# reaction's id therefore travels in an extra `redacts` key, while `message_ts`/`reaction` stay
# empty (a redaction does not name the target message or the emoji; resolving them would cost an
# extra room-event fetch per redaction). `item_user_id` is left empty for the same reason — the
# reacted-to message's author is not known synchronously, and no in-tree consumer reads the field
# (`grep -rn item_user_id` -> base.py's contract docstring and Slack's producer only). Redactions
# of ordinary message events are forwarded too; telling them apart is the consumer's job.
#
# Considered and rejected:
#   (1) Driving reminders through the built-in picker prompt machinery (_MatrixPickerPrompt /
#       send_choice_picker): its registry is in-memory and expires at MATRIX_APPROVAL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
#       (default 300s) — _claim_reaction_prompt -> _matrix_prompt_expired -> on_expired consumes the
#       reaction and runs nothing, so a tap even ten minutes after the reminder would be answered
#       "expired". Widening that timeout is the wrong fix: it also governs exec-approval prompts,
#       where a day-long window is a security regression.
#   (2) Polling /sync from a side process to collect reactions: a second sync loop plus a
#       token/cursor store for no benefit over the push path.
#   (3) Relying on the prompt registry with only a longer expires_at: the registry lives in memory
#       (MemoryStateStore/MemorySyncStore), so a gateway restart drops every pending prompt.
#
# Runtime files only: plugins/platforms/matrix/adapter.py
#
# Verified 2026-10-12 against a pristine v0.21.6 checkout (818c13be): a real `patch -p1` on a fresh
# copy of the file rc=0, no fuzz, no .rej/.orig; `patch -p1 -R --dry-run` rc=0; and every check
# below was run on that applied result — `py_compile` clean; `ruff check` clean under the repo's
# own pyproject.toml; the added lines are `ruff format`-clean (they do not appear in
# `ruff format --diff` on the patched file — the pristine file already fails `ruff format --check`
# wholesale on its own pre-existing style, so that check carries no signal here); AST shape via the
# image repo's scripts/check_structure.py: MatrixAdapter 125 -> 127 methods (_forward_reaction_event,
# _on_redaction), one new top-level function (_matrix_raw_event), no class-body indent drift.
#
# Re-anchor on a base bump: the patch is two insertions (the add_event_handler block in _connect,
# the tail of _on_reaction) plus one new method pair anchored on `_claim_reaction_prompt`. If those
# anchors move, re-anchor; drop it only if upstream starts forwarding reactions itself.
diff --git a/plugins/platforms/matrix/adapter.py b/plugins/platforms/matrix/adapter.py
